@startuml
title AsyncSharedState — dynamic allocation and endpoint ownership

actor "Task/API caller" as Caller
participant "makeAsyncState<T>(allocator)" as Factory
participant "Allocator" as Allocator
participant "AsyncSharedState<T>" as State
participant "AsyncPromise<T>" as Promise
participant "AsyncFuture<T>" as Future
participant "Awaiting coroutine" as Awaiter

Caller -> Factory : create state pair
Factory -> Allocator : allocate sizeof(AsyncSharedState<T>)
Allocator --> Factory : storage or allocation error
alt allocation succeeds
  Factory -> State : construct in allocated storage
  Factory -> Promise : create move-only endpoint
  Factory -> Future : create move-only endpoint
  Factory --> Caller : AsyncStatePair<T>
else allocation fails
  Factory --> Caller : Result error
end

Caller -> Promise : set value / signal completion
Promise -> State : publish result and transition completion state
opt waiter already registered
  State -> Awaiter : schedule through selected executor
end
Awaiter -> Future : consume result

note over State,Future
  The state is reference-counted while endpoints, await operations, and
  completion callbacks use it. The factory retains the allocator's
  deallocation operation so the storage is released correctly.
  AsyncSharedState is not a user-created stack object.
end note

note over Promise,Future
  AsyncPromise and AsyncFuture are move-only. A custom allocator may choose
  the placement strategy for the shared state; it does not relocate the
  coroutine frame itself.
end note
@enduml
